  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 1.4. Strengthened social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Following the needs and priority assessment consultations with the tripartite partners, national tripartite dialogue between representatives of SEFOPE (Secretary of State for Vocational Training and Employment), CCI-TL (Chamber of Commerce and Industry Timor-Leste) and KSTL (Trade Union Confederation Timor-Leste) led to identification and consensus on reform agenda for dispute resolution system (on 18 August 2021). The Dialogue concluded with the following recommendations:
-	Bipartite level: develop guidelines on dispute resolution at enterprise level and establish national bipartite forum for dialogue, relationship building and promoting cooperation to achieve mutual objectives;
-	Strengthen mediation and conciliation services and capacity of SEFOPE (proposals include legislative initiative for strengthening mediation as dispute resolution mechanism, development of procedural guidelines for mediators and provision of training for the existing mediators by Judicial Training Center);
-	Amendment of Law No. 4 of 2012 to strengthen role of Tripartite Arbitration Council and implementation of its decisions &#x2013; proposals in this regard included specific law on arbitration, dialogue between the Arbitration Council and District Court to strengthen implementation of processes and decisions).
As a follow-up, draft guidelines for grievance handling mechanism (adapted from IRToolkit) were developed and will be discussed in a bipartite dialogue in October 2021. [The results will be confirmed after the workshops]

 The focus of ILO interventions has been on labour dispute resolution and workplace cooperation for productivity and decent work.
ILO has produced a number of outputs to support this tripartite and bipartite dialogue process such as review of dispute resolution policy and mechanism in Timor Leste, COVID-19 response communication materials (posters and banners that promote health insurance, job security in the workplace and advocate for collective bargaining to protect the interest of workers and employers in Timor-Leste) and capacity building of employers and workers organization on issues connected with social dialogue and labour relations. 
The ILO also carried out a need and priority assessment of tripartite constituents; built capacity of Employers (CCITL) and  Workers organization (KSTL); and conducted tripartite dialogue on dispute resolution mechanism with a view to identify areas of improvement.</narrative>
   </description>
